%I A100409 #19 Nov 25 2026 22:16:35
%S A100409 48,68,78,88,98
%N A100409 Numbers m such that m is d_1*d_2*...*d_k-th composite number (m=A002808(d_1*d_2*...*d_k)) where d_1 d_2 ... d_k is the decimal expansion of m.
%C A100409 If m is a composite number then A002808(m - pi(m) - 1) = m.
%C A100409 These are the fixed points of the map c -> A002808(A007954(c)) on the composite numbers (a number with a zero digit has no image). The only other cycle is 6 -> 12 -> 6. There are no further cycles: a zerofree composite with 8 or more digits maps to a smaller composite, and all composites below 10^7 were checked. - _Osman Hassan Hussein_, Nov 10 2026
%e A100409 98 is in the sequence because 9*8=72 and 98 is 72nd composite number (98=A002808(9*8)).
%e A100409 Other composites reach these fixed points: 84 -> A002808(32) = 48; 86 -> A002808(48) = 68; 87 -> A002808(56) = 78; 188 -> A002808(64) = 88; 189 -> A002808(72) = 98.
%t A100409 Do[h=IntegerDigits[n];l=Length[h];If[ !PrimeQ[n]&& n-PrimePi[n]-1==Product[h[[k]], {k, l}], v=Append[v, n]; Print[n]], {n, 100000}]
%Y A100409 Cf. A002808, A007954, A097223 (prime analog).
%K A100409 base,fini,full,nonn,changed
%O A100409 1,1
%A A100409 _Farideh Firoozbakht_, Dec 06 2004
